h the algorithm that we give in the  realizable case  but it remains an interesting open  question to determine the exact  dependency  of decay all right so that's the  realizable setting let us now move on to  the agnostic setting  for the agnostic running time lower  bound you need  a different and new complexity theoretic  hypothesis  to state it let us recall the densest  case sub graph or dks  problem where we are given an undirected  graph t  on n vertices and a positive integer k  and the goal is to find k vertex  subgraph of d  with maximum number of edges  our hypothesis which we call gap eth for  density k subgraph or gravitation for  dks  states that no 2 to the leader of n time  algorithm  can approximate the in this case of  graph to within  a constant factor now  honestly this is a very strong  hypothesis but it  also seems somewhat plausible and one  reason is that  if you assume that certain random csps  are hard to refute in sub-exponential  time  then the gap eth for dks would follow  on the other hand the bad news is that  no np harness of approximation for dks  is  known even for say 1.1  factor so it seems very hard to prove  and possibly out of reach of the current  techniques  okay so using this admittedly strong  assumption  of gap eth for dks we can show that  even for agnostic training of one relu  with additive error epsilon  it cannot be done in time exponential in  little  of 1 over epsilon square times poly and  k  now one way epsilon square here in the  exponent is  tight because it matches with our  algorithm and moreover  this give us separation between agnostic  and realizable  learning because in the latter we can  get it  in exponential in one over epsilon if k  is constant so those are  all our results for the rest of this  talk i will go through  each of them and describe at a high  level how it's proved  we'll start with the algorithms so i  copy the results here on the right  and i also copy the definition of k relu  here on t