at  in the intended solution  since we set all coordinate i for which  t i belongs to the set cover to be one  we have ensured that in this sum at  least one of the term is one  so the left hand side will be zero and  this constraint will always be satisfied  in the intended solution the second type  of constraints that we'll be adding  are the subset constraints so for each  subset  t i will create a constraint that  relu of delta minus w i  is equals to delta where delta is some  small positive real number  the point here is that this constraint  is satisfied  only when the view is less than or equal  to zero  in other words it is violated when ti  is selected in the set cover  now due to time constraint we will not  be going through the  entire proof here but the rough idea is  that  the element constraint ensures that the  selected set is a valid set cover  whereas the subset constraint ensures  that we are  picking as few set as possible and hence  this is indeed the set cover problem  all right so that's conclude the high  level overview of the reduction and the  proof  that training a single relu is np hard  in the agnostic case  before we go forward let me note here  that this proof doesn't directly  give the hardness of approximation uh to  within almost polynomial factor  yet and the reason is that set cover  itself can be  approximate within log m factor  so to get the claim hardness  approximation  we have to reduce from a different  problem which is a generalization of the  set cover  problem called the minimum monotone  circuit satisfiability  this problem is known to be hard to  approximate to almost polynomial  factor and by generalizing this  reduction  we can prove a similar statement for the  agnostic training of a single relu  all right so now that we are done with  the agnostic case let us now move on to  the  realizable case and i'm copying the  definition of k relu here for  convenience  it turns out that in this case wu had  already  proved a